[section:spheroidal Spheroidal Wave Functions]

[h4 Synopsis]

    #include <boost/math/special_functions/spheroidal.hpp>

    namespace boost { namespace math {

    // Eigenvalues λ_n^m(c²) and λ_n^m(-c²):
    template <class T>
    ``__sf_result`` prolate_spheroidal_eigenvalue(unsigned n, unsigned m, T c);
    template <class T>
    ``__sf_result`` oblate_spheroidal_eigenvalue(unsigned n, unsigned m, T c);

    // Angular functions Ps_n^m(η, ±c²):
    template <class T1, class T2>
    ``__sf_result`` prolate_spheroidal_angular(unsigned n, unsigned m, T1 c, T2 eta, ``__sf_result``* derivative = nullptr);
    template <class T1, class T2>
    ``__sf_result`` oblate_spheroidal_angular(unsigned n, unsigned m, T1 c, T2 eta, ``__sf_result``* derivative = nullptr);

    // Radial functions of the first and second kind:
    template <class T1, class T2>
    ``__sf_result`` prolate_spheroidal_radial_1(unsigned n, unsigned m, T1 c, T2 xi, ``__sf_result``* derivative = nullptr);
    template <class T1, class T2>
    ``__sf_result`` prolate_spheroidal_radial_2(unsigned n, unsigned m, T1 c, T2 xi, ``__sf_result``* derivative = nullptr);
    template <class T1, class T2>
    ``__sf_result`` oblate_spheroidal_radial_1(unsigned n, unsigned m, T1 c, T2 xi, ``__sf_result``* derivative = nullptr);
    template <class T1, class T2>
    ``__sf_result`` oblate_spheroidal_radial_2(unsigned n, unsigned m, T1 c, T2 xi, ``__sf_result``* derivative = nullptr);

    // All of the above for one (n, m, c), at many arguments:
    template <class Real, class Policy = policies::policy<>>
    class prolate_spheroidal
    {
    public:
        prolate_spheroidal(unsigned n, unsigned m, Real c);
        Real eigenvalue() const;
        Real angular(Real eta, Real* derivative = nullptr) const;
        Real radial_1(Real xi, Real* derivative = nullptr) const;
        Real radial_2(Real xi, Real* derivative = nullptr) const;
    };
    template <class Real, class Policy = policies::policy<>>
    class oblate_spheroidal; // the same members

    }} // namespaces

Each function also has an overload taking a trailing __Policy argument (after the derivative pointer).
When `derivative` is not null it receives the derivative with respect to [eta] or [xi]; the two share the expensive
part of the computation, so asking for both costs little more than the value alone. The derivatives are computed
from forms that avoid the cancellation an automatic differentiation of the value would inherit near [xi] = 1.

Most of the cost of each call, 15-30 [mu]s in double, is the eigenvalue and the expansion coefficients, which depend
only on /n/, /m/ and /c/. The classes compute them once, in the constructor, and their members return exactly what
the free functions do, so evaluating at many arguments is up to 20 times faster:

    boost::math::prolate_spheroidal<double> f(4, 1, 16.0);
    for (double xi : grid)
    {
        double d;
        double R2 = f.radial_2(xi, &d);
    }

The members are `const` and an object may be shared between threads. Domain errors in /n/, /m/ or /c/ are raised by the constructor.

[h4 Description]

Separating the Helmholtz equation in prolate or oblate spheroidal coordinates gives the angular equation
([@https://dlmf.nist.gov/30.2.E1 DLMF 30.2.1])

[expression d/d[eta]((1-[eta][super 2]) dw/d[eta]) + ([lambda] + [gamma][super 2](1-[eta][super 2]) - m[super 2]/(1-[eta][super 2])) w = 0]

with [gamma][super 2] = c[super 2] for prolate and [gamma][super 2] = -c[super 2] for oblate spheroids,
together with a radial equation of the same form. For each order /m/ and degree /n/ >= /m/ the equation
has a solution bounded at [eta] = [plusminus]1 only when [lambda] is the eigenvalue [lambda][sub n][super m]([gamma][super 2])
([@https://dlmf.nist.gov/30.3 DLMF 30.3]), which `prolate_spheroidal_eigenvalue` and `oblate_spheroidal_eigenvalue` return.

The angular functions are DLMF's Ps[sub n][super m]([eta], [gamma][super 2]) ([@https://dlmf.nist.gov/30.4 DLMF 30.4]):
they are normalized like the Ferrers functions __legendre_p, with the same Condon-Shortley phase,

[expression [int][sub -1][super 1] Ps[sub n][super m]([eta], [gamma][super 2])[super 2] d[eta] = 2(n+m)! / ((2n+1)(n-m)!)]

and their sign makes Ps[sub n][super m](0) (for even /n/ - /m/) or its derivative at 0 (for odd /n/ - /m/)
have the sign of the Ferrers function's. At /c/ = 0 they reduce to the Ferrers functions P[sub n][super m]([eta]).

The radial functions are Flammer's R[sub mn][super (1)] and R[sub mn][super (2)], DLMF's S[sub n][super m(1)] and S[sub n][super m(2)]
([@https://dlmf.nist.gov/30.11 DLMF 30.11]) for the prolate case, normalized to behave like the spherical
Bessel functions j[sub n](c[xi]) and y[sub n](c[xi]) as [xi] [rarr] [infin]. The prolate functions are defined for [xi] >= 1
and the oblate ones for [xi] >= 0. Their Wronskian is

[expression R[super (1)] R[super (2)]' - R[super (1)]' R[super (2)] = 1 / (c([xi][super 2] - 1)) (prolate), 1 / (c([xi][super 2] + 1)) (oblate)]


[h4 Relation to other conventions]

Flammer, Abramowitz and Stegun, and `scipy.special` use the eigenvalue [lambda] + [gamma][super 2]:
`pro_cv(m, n, c)` is `prolate_spheroidal_eigenvalue(n, m, c) + c*c` and `obl_cv(m, n, c)` is
`oblate_spheroidal_eigenvalue(n, m, c) - c*c`. Their angular functions (`pro_ang1`, `obl_ang1`) use
Flammer's normalization, S[sub mn](c, 0) = P[sub n][super m](0) (or the same for the derivatives when /n/ - /m/ is odd),
without the Condon-Shortley phase; they are (-1)[super m] P[sub n][super m](0) / Ps[sub n][super m](0) times ours. Flammer's normalization
is avoided here because for oblate functions with large /c/ the function's value at 0 is exponentially small
compared with its norm: at /c/ = 60 Flammer-normalized oblate functions have norms near 10[super 24].
The radial functions agree in all of these conventions.

[h4 Domain]

/c/ may be negative: the eigenvalues and angular functions depend only on /c/[super 2], and the radial functions of the first and
second kinds are multiplied by (-1)[super /n/] and (-1)[super /n/+1] when the sign of /c/ changes, as in Mathematica.
/n/ < /m/, |[eta]| > 1, [xi] < 1 (prolate) or [xi] < 0 (oblate), non-finite arguments,
and /c/ = 0 for the radial functions are domain errors.
The prolate radial functions of the second kind, and the derivative of the first kind for /m/ = 1, are infinite at [xi] = 1
and raise an overflow error there, as does a request for the derivative of the angular function at [eta] = [plusminus]1 for /m/ = 1.

[h4 Accuracy]

Errors measured against 384-bit references (Arb via Nemo, with the radial functions checked against their Wronskian
to 40 digits) on a grid with /c/ <= 50, /m/ <= 5, /n/ - /m/ <= 10, scaled by the local envelope max(|f|, |x f'|, |f'|/k)
so that zeros of these oscillating functions do not dominate, are at most

[table
[[Function] [prolate, c <= 8] [prolate, c <= 50] [oblate, c <= 8] [oblate, c <= 50]]
[[eigenvalue] [1 [epsilon]] [1 [epsilon]] [1 [epsilon]] [1 [epsilon]]]
[[angular] [6 [epsilon]] [16 [epsilon]] [10 [epsilon]] [15 [epsilon]]]
[[angular derivative] [26 [epsilon]] [36 [epsilon]] [51 [epsilon]] [49 [epsilon]]]
[[radial, first kind] [3 [epsilon]] [4 [epsilon]] [6 [epsilon]] [10 [epsilon]]]
[[radial, second kind] [2 [epsilon]] [3 [epsilon]] [6 [epsilon]] [25 [epsilon]]]
]

The eigenvalues are correctly rounded in all but a few cases (at most 0.84 ulp).
Where the angular functions are exponentially small (oblate, near [eta] = 0, large /c/) they are also ill-conditioned
in /c/: at /c/ = 30, |c [part]Ps/[part]c / Ps| [asymp] 28 at [eta] = 0, so errors of a few [epsilon] there are within the conditioning.
For [xi] - 1 down to 10[super -6] the prolate radial functions stay within a few [epsilon], and the second kind within a few times
its condition number |([xi] - 1)R'/R| in [xi] - 1 (which is exact for a double [xi] near 1),
and for large c[xi] the radial functions oscillate rapidly, so their condition number |[xi]R'/R| is at least about c[xi].
At large [xi] the phase c[radic]([xi][super 2] - [tau]) is carried in two parts, so the radial functions stay within a few times
their condition number.
On the same grid `scipy.special.pro_rad2` and `obl_rad2` (SciPy 1.18) have no correct digits at many points.
With `cpp_bin_float_50` the radial functions of the first kind are accurate to a few [epsilon] and those of the second kind to within about a hundred.

[h4 Implementation]

The eigenvalue is bracketed by Sturm bisection on the symmetrized tridiagonal matrix of the three-term recurrence
for the Legendre expansion coefficients d[sub k] (Abramowitz and Stegun 21.7.3) and polished with Newton's method
on Bouwkamp's continued fraction. The coefficients come from the continued fractions, matched at the largest |d[sub k]|
so that both run in their stable directions.
For large /c/ the eigenvalue and the denominators of these continued fractions are small differences of terms of size c[super 2],
so rounding the eigenvalue alone costs an absolute error of about c[super 2][epsilon]: 17 ulps of the eigenvalue, 1000 [epsilon] in
the low-order d[sub k] at /c/ = 50, and a bias of several [epsilon] in solutions integrated where they grow exponentially. As Arb does with
guard bits, the step that amplifies the rounding is repeated at higher precision: Bouwkamp's residual at the rounded eigenvalue,
in double length, gives its low part by one Newton step, and the continued fractions for the d[sub k] are rerun in double length
with the eigenvalue to that precision. The integrations use the low part in the coefficient [lambda] - s[eta][super 2] of the equation.

The angular functions are sums of Ferrers functions. Where that sum cancels, near [eta] = 0 for oblate and near
[eta] = [plusminus]1 for prolate functions with large /c/, the angular equation is integrated by Taylor series from [eta] = 0
(by parity) or from the regular solution's power series at [eta] = 1, in the direction in which the function grows,
and matched to the sum where it is accurate. The same integration fixes the sign of the oblate functions when
their value at 0 is too small to determine.

For the radial functions of the first kind the classic expansion in j[sub m+k](c[xi]) is normalized by
[sum] d[sub k](2m+k)!/k!, which cancels catastrophically for prolate functions with large /c/ (at /c/ = 50 to 16 digits).
The prolate functions use instead the expansion in j[sub m+k](c[radic]([xi][super 2]-1)) normalized by Flammer's sum,
as Van Buren and Boisvert do; for oblate functions the roles are reversed, and the classic expansion is used.

The radial functions of the second kind come from the expansion in spherical Neumann functions where it converges
without cancellation (small /c/), and otherwise from the asymptotic expansion of R[super (1)] + iR[super (2)] in powers of 1/[xi]
with the phase c[radic]([xi][super 2] - [tau]), [tau] = [plusminus]1 - [lambda]/c[super 2], chosen to match the local wavenumber so that its coefficients
do not grow with /c/. Either is evaluated at the nearest point where it is accurate and integrated inward by
Taylor series, the direction in which the second-kind function dominates.
The integration's rounding adds a few [epsilon] times R[super (1)], which near [xi] = 1, where R[super (1)] is several times R[super (2)],
is far outside the conditioning in [xi] - 1. There (prolate, /c/ > 8, [xi] - 1 < 1/8) it runs in double length,
from the asymptotic expansion at [xi] = min(1 + c/8, 4); this costs up to five times as much.

Near [xi] = 0 (for [xi] < min(1, 4/c)) the oblate R[super (2)] is a combination A u[sub e] + B u[sub o] of the even and odd
solutions at 0. The Wronskian gives the coefficient of the solution with the parity of Ps[sub n][super m] exactly. The other one,
R[super (2)](0) or R[super (2)]'(0), is exponentially small for large /c/ and is lost to cancellation by any inward integration.
For large /c/ the oblate eigenvalues come in pairs, degrees /n/ and /n/ [plusminus] 1, split by an exponentially small
[Delta] = [lambda][sub o] - [lambda][sub e]. With f = R[super (2)] of degree /n/ and g the first-kind function of its partner, the radial equation
gives d/d[xi][([xi][super 2]+1) W(f, g)] = ([lambda][sub g] - [lambda][sub f]) f g, and W vanishes at infinity, so the coefficient is
-[Delta] [int][sub 0][super [infin]] f g d[xi] divided by g(0) or g'(0). The same identity for the angular functions on [0, 1] gives
[Delta] = Ps[sub e](0) Ps[sub o]'(0) / [int][sub 0][super 1] Ps[sub e] Ps[sub o] d[eta] to full relative accuracy.
These are computed by an exact product of Taylor series out to [xi] = max(1, (10 - 0.6 ln [epsilon])/c), and beyond that
from the asymptotic expansion: the slowly varying part term by term, the oscillating part by repeated integration by parts.

[h4 References]

* C. Flammer, ['Spheroidal Wave Functions], Stanford University Press, 1957.
* NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, [@https://dlmf.nist.gov/30 Chapter 30].
* A. L. Van Buren and J. E. Boisvert, ['Accurate calculation of prolate spheroidal radial functions of the first kind and their first derivatives], Quart. Appl. Math. 60 (2002) 589-599.
* A. L. Van Buren and J. E. Boisvert, ['Improved calculation of prolate spheroidal radial functions of the second kind and their first derivatives], Quart. Appl. Math. 62 (2004) 493-507.

[endsect] [/section:spheroidal Spheroidal Wave Functions]
